WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.490
Rob Schneider was everywhere in the late 1990s
and the early 2000s.

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:08.710
But since then, he's become a bit of a phantom,
only popping up in films and on TV on the

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:09.710
rarest of occasions.

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:13.180
If you think it's because Hollywood is avoiding
him, you might be right.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:16.230
Here's why Hollywood won't cast Rob Schneider
anymore.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:17.980
Box office beatdown

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:21.970
Not counting the Adam Sandler movies he's
appeared in, Schneider's flicks are anything

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:25.779
but critical darlings.

00:00:25.779 --> 00:00:30.380
His best-reviewed film, The Animal, only holds
a 30 percent rating on Rotten Tomatoes, and

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:33.370
it earned him a Razzie nomination for Worst
Actor of the Decade.

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:36.430
At least Schneider's films tend to be one-note,
low-budget affairs…

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:42.539
“Rob Schneider is a Wall Street executive
with everything going for him.

00:00:42.539 --> 00:00:47.179
Only problem is, he’s about to become...a
carrot!.”

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:48.940
“I’m a carrot!”

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:52.140
As a result, almost every single one of them
earned back their budgets.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.480
Still, studios don't tend to be interested
in mid-level hits.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:58.749
Hiring Rob Schneider is like buying store
brand when you could be eating real Count

00:00:58.749 --> 00:01:00.390
Chocula for fifty cents more.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.300
Do you really come out ahead?

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:03.739
Discount bin material

00:01:03.739 --> 00:01:07.810
While most people are aware of the Deuce Bigalow
movies and The Hot Chick, many don't realize

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:13.380
Schneider actually continued to make films...probably
because most of them went straight to DVD.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:17.100
In 2007, he directed and starred in Big Stan,
followed by The Chosen One in 2010.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:18.290
“The chosen one?”

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:20.380
Yeah, we’ve never heard of them either.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.930
Even Norm of the North, in which Schneider
voiced the main character, was regarded as

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:24.930
a bomb.

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:26.350
Is Schneider the touch of death?

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:30.860
We're not saying he definitely is, but please,
if you do see Rob...don't let him touch you.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:32.950
“Lisa, shut your eyes.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:34.260
Soon you will be at peace."

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:35.549
"Hey, quit it Bart.

00:01:35.549 --> 00:01:36.549
Quit it!

00:01:36.549 --> 00:01:37.549
Hey, quit it!

00:01:37.549 --> 00:01:39.460
Quit it quit it quit it Mooooom!”

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:40.460
Lawsuit magnet

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:43.790
Even though Schneider's movies tend to make
their money back, sometimes the profits don't

00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:45.370
end up where they're supposed to go.

00:01:45.370 --> 00:01:50.009
Investors actually sued him for his 2010 straight-to-DVD
release The Chosen One in an attempt to recoup

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:51.009
their money.

00:01:51.009 --> 00:01:54.939
While lawsuits in Hollywood aren't uncommon,
when you take into account the lack of blockbusters

00:01:54.939 --> 00:01:59.250
in Schneider's portfolio, inviting lawsuits
is really just one more reason not to hire

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:00.250
him.

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:01.250
He's a bit player

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:04.840
Rob Schneider may have headlined a few movies,
but plenty of movie fans find it hard to name

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.439
the ones that don't have Deuce Bigalow in
the title.

00:02:07.439 --> 00:02:10.819
And yet, he's gained international fame for
his smaller roles, like the Cajun guy from

00:02:10.819 --> 00:02:14.019
Adam Sandler's The Waterboy, in which he repeated
one sentence.

00:02:14.019 --> 00:02:15.019
Several times.

00:02:15.019 --> 00:02:16.080
“You can do it.”

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.080
...and which he repeated in at least five
of Sandler's movies.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.000
“You can do it.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.500
You can do it.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:23.970
You can do it.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:24.970
YOU can do it.”

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:28.599
Just for that, you could argue Schneider is
little more than a one-hit wonder.

00:02:28.599 --> 00:02:30.110
“Ashley, makin’ copies.”

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:31.840
Okay, maybe two hits.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.159
Junk science activist

00:02:33.159 --> 00:02:37.290
Activism in Hollywood is nothing new, but
when an actor weighs in on a hot-button issue,

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.580
it's reasonable to expect that it can have
an impact on their employment opportunities.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:44.590
This has definitely been the case for Schneider,
whose outspoken opposition to vaccines, and

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:47.879
support of a debunked theory linking them
to autism, cost him his gig as a pitchman

00:02:47.879 --> 00:02:49.569
for State Farm in 2014.

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:52.980
“There’s an unnecessary shot that they
don’t need to have, especially when the

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.530
baby’s immune system isn’t developed.”

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:57.920
Tweeting a George Washington quote about freedom
of speech wasn't enough to get him his job

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:02.040
back, but Schneider has kept up his anti-vaccine
crusade, claiming to be in possession of a

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.290
"smoking gun" report proving they're harmful.

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:08.620
Later, he was accused of leaving a threatening
message for a California politician, Lorena

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:12.269
Gonzalez, who was working on behalf of a mandatory
vaccine legislation.

00:03:12.269 --> 00:03:14.580
And you thought Schnieder was no laughing
matter before.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:17.519
The assemblywoman responded on her Facebook
page, saying, quote:

00:03:17.519 --> 00:03:21.430
"...let’s be honest … that is 20 mins
of my life I’ll never get back arguing that

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:25.379
vaccines don’t cause autism with Deuce Bigalow,
male gigolo.‪"

00:03:25.379 --> 00:03:27.140
He's behind a microphone

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:30.530
More recently, Rob Schneider has disappeared
from the screen to lend his vocal talents

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:33.690
to animated features instead, like the aforementioned
Norm of the North.

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:37.701
Dino Time and The Little Penguin Pororo's
Racing Adventure didn't fare too well, so

00:03:37.701 --> 00:03:40.650
Schneider's obviously making some rough voicework
choices.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:44.000
So while his face has become a rare sight
on the silver screen, we'll probably hear

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:45.400
more from him in the future.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:49.250
Even if it is in the sequel to the Kung-Fu
Panda ripoff, The Adventures of Panda Warrior.

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:51.140
No, seriously, that's a thing.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:53.370
“Let’s go rescue Princess Angelica.

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:56.340
Pigs really can fly!”

00:03:56.349 --> 00:03:57.900
If only we were making this up.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:01.159
There’s like three different Kung Fu Panda
ripoff movies.

00:04:01.159 --> 00:04:02.379
Ethnic stereotypes

00:04:02.379 --> 00:04:05.650
Watch any movie featuring Rob Schneider, and
odds are good you'll see him playing some

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:10.439
kind of ethnic caricature, like the Hawaiian
Ula from 50 First Dates or the Asian minister

00:04:10.439 --> 00:04:12.299
from I Now Pronounce You Chuck and Larry.

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:13.460
'Ho!

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:15.680
Hoho!"

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:19.411
With whitewashing controversies becoming more
of a problem for Hollywood in general, it

00:04:19.411 --> 00:04:23.000
makes sense he'd have fewer opportunities
to do just that — especially considering

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.890
he's been accused of mugging to the point
of ridicule.

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:26.890
Self-employed

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:30.540
Like a lot of other creatives, Schneider is
leaving traditional film and television broadcasting

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:31.540
behind.

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:35.260
Instead, he's taking his talent to the Internet,
where he can do pretty much whatever he wants.

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:39.180
In addition to a part in his pal Adam Sandler's
critically-despised Netflix film The Ridiculous

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:43.720
6, Schneider released a stand-up special Soy
Sauce and the Holocaust in 2013, as well as

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:46.030
his self-produced series Real Rob in 2015.

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:49.980
At this point, studios can rest easy knowing
they don't have to worry about hiring Schneider

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:50.980
anymore.

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:52.350
These days, he just employs himself.

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:54.380
Not totally hopeless

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:57.910
Despite not appearing in theaters all that
often anymore, Rob Schneider's career probably

00:04:57.910 --> 00:04:58.910
isn't over.

00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:01.470
He's keeping busy, and a comeback of some
kind is still possible.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:04.580
Look, if Mel Gibson can do it, just about
anybody can.

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:07.010
As long as we never see another Deuce Bigalow
movie.

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:09.590
Either way, he seems okay with where he's
at.

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:15.310
“Here’s my economics: I’m gonna go out
to my $150,000 car, drive back to my $2 million

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:18.000
house, and hang out with my hot 29-year-old
wife.”

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:19.470
Can't really argue with that.

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:20.720
Thanks for watching!

